TOPIC: jak działa zszywacz biurowy
OUTPUT LANGUAGE: Polish (pl)
TARGET DURATION: 30 seconds
NARRATION WORD RANGE: 42-66 words
TARGET NARRATION WORD COUNT: exactly 54 words; never exceed the allowed range and never pad or distort meaning to hit the target
SPOKEN SENTENCE CONTRACT: the joined narration MUST contain at least 3 natural complete content sentences in every output language. A standalone closure such as Koniec., The end., Конец., or Кінець. is filler, does not count as a content sentence, and must not be used. Scene cuts remain independent of sentence boundaries.
EXACT SCENE COUNT: 9
EXACT TOTAL SHOT COUNT: 9
TARGET WORD ITEMS PER SCENE S1..Sn (GUIDANCE): [6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,6]
Output each narration as narration_words: one lexical word per array item, with punctuation attached; never put spaces or a whole sentence in an item. Code joins the items into natural continuous narration.

OUTPUT CONTRACT:
{"scenes":[{"scene_id":"S1","narration_words":["One","word","per","item."],"evidence_ids":["E1"],"shots":[{"shot_id":"S1-A","visual_intent":"concrete visible subject/action/mechanism in English","must_show":["concrete required visual concept"],"must_not_show":["specific conflicting visual if relevant"],"queries_en":["precise technical query","broader physical fallback query","encyclopedic synonym query"],"preferred_media_type":"photo"}]}]}

RULES:
- the continuous narration is formed by joining narration_words in scene order; do not output separate narration strings;
- scene_id values must be sequential S1, S2, S3...;
- every scene must cite 1-4 valid evidence_ids from the supplied list;
- each scene must contain exactly 1 shot;
- For 30-second videos, the final scene narration must contain at least 6 spoken words. Keep the complete narration natural and evidence-supported; move visual scene cuts between existing nearby words when necessary, without adding filler or changing speech speed.
- each scene narration must be an exact contiguous segment of the continuous narration with 2-14 words; scene boundaries MAY occur inside a sentence because they are visual cut points, so a segment may start lowercase or end with comma/no terminal punctuation; the joined scene narrations must form natural complete narration that starts normally and ends with . ! ? or …;
- if a scene narration segment begins with a relative/anaphoric word such as which/that/it, który/która/które, который/которая/которое, який/яка/яке, first resolve what noun/result the pronoun actually refers to; do NOT assume it refers to the previous shot primary when the previous narration introduced another referent after that primary; if it does refer to the previous visual primary, keep that primary unless the current segment explicitly names a new concrete primary; never switch to an object unrelated to the actual referent/current clause;
- visual metadata must be grounded in the current narration segment plus the immediately preceding unfinished clause when the scene cut occurs inside one sentence;
- shot_id must start with its scene_id, for example S2-A or S2-B;
- each shot must have a concrete visual_intent, at least one must_show concept, and exactly 3 distinct English queries;
- CRITICAL LANGUAGE SPLIT: narration/scenes use OUTPUT LANGUAGE, but ALL visual metadata fields (visual_intent, every must_show item, every must_not_show item, and every queries_en item) MUST be written in English only regardless of OUTPUT LANGUAGE;
- never translate visual metadata into Polish, Russian, or Ukrainian; only spoken narration uses the requested non-English language;
- visual_intent must prefer a directly searchable real physical subject/result; for a specialized process, show the exact equipment if common, otherwise show the immediate physical input/output that proves the step;
- if visual_intent requires the visible ends, tips, heads, or edges of an independently attached object on a surface, include BOTH that object and the surface in must_show; a surface anchor alone does not require the attached object. Keep the visible shape and orientation in visual_intent.
- must_show must contain 1-3 SHORT independent English visual anchors, each 1-3 words; do not put a whole sentence/action phrase in one must_show item;
- must_show[0] must identify the concrete main subject of the narration: use the complete common name of the whole object, organism, equipment, place, or visible material; never a detached body part, generic droplet/texture, action, or hidden reaction when the scene is about a larger identifiable subject;
- if a proposed must_show[0] is only a generic component label such as base, unit, part, module, assembly, section, piece, or detail OF an identifiable concrete owner object, use that owner object as must_show[0]; keep the component detail only in visual_intent and detailed queries, and use the owner object as the robust query-3 fallback;
- prefer ONE precise identifying must_show anchor when one visible subject is sufficient. When visual_intent requires a visible interaction, measurement, connection, comparison, transfer, or spatial relationship between TWO independently visible physical subjects, include the second required subject as must_show[1]. Do not omit it merely because must_show[0] names the main instrument/object. Every supplied anchor is a requirement, not an optional keyword.
- secondary must_show anchors must add an independent visible subject/context; never restate, synonymize, or describe a component/detail of must_show[0] (for example water turbine + turbine blades);
- if must_show has two required visible subjects, queries_en[0] and queries_en[1] must contain distinctive terms for BOTH subjects; query 3 may remain the robust primary-subject fallback;
- when the narrated process is invisible inside an object or organism, show that same object or organism in relevant physical context. For photo-only output, never request an interior/internal/inside/within view of a compact object unless visual_intent explicitly says open, cutaway, exposed, transparent, or disassembled; otherwise keep hidden parts out of visual_intent and queries. Do not invent a visibly different substitute that merely shares a texture or broad scientific category.
- when a flow/current/reaction happens through or inside a CLOSED conduit or machine, make that conduit/machine the primary must_show; do not require hidden contents/process to be visible unless visual_intent explicitly says transparent/open/cutaway/exposed.
- avoid generic secondary anchors when a more specific visible subject is available, but do not invent equipment that is not supported by the narration or evidence;
- must_show[0] MUST be a concrete searchable physical subject, material, object, place, equipment item, or visible result that should actually appear in the frame; never use only an abstract process, action, or generic descriptor as must_show[0];
- visual_intent MUST contain must_show[0] verbatim as one contiguous English phrase; do not replace the primary phrase with a synonym or alternate technical name;
- put optional context, process, location, material, result, or equipment anchors after the primary physical subject;
- queries_en items 1 and 2 must contain at least one distinctive term from must_show[0]; query 3 is the robust physical-subject fallback and must center must_show[0];
- queries_en must contain exactly 3 distinct English queries: (1) precise process/equipment anchored to must_show[0], (2) broader process/result query anchored to must_show[0], (3) must_show[0] itself as a short 1-4 word physical-subject fallback; process specificity belongs in queries 1 and 2;
- at least two of the three queries must contain a distinctive term from the primary subject or a specific contextual anchor, not only generic words;
- avoid the word photo in queries unless it is part of an established proper phrase; search nouns are more important than presentation words;
- must_show must describe directly visible objects, equipment, materials, attributes, actions, or physical results that can be verified in a real photo/video;
- each English query must be concise, factual, concrete, and 2-10 words and must describe what should literally be visible;
- avoid generic filler queries such as beautiful/stunning scenery, nature background, wallpaper, or broad topic-only searches;
- preferred_media_type must be photo only; use a real photograph of the exact object, equipment, material, place, or physical result described;
- prioritize semantic accuracy over motion; do not request video in this production mode;
- when narration describes an invisible mechanism, choose a real physical object, machine, experiment, process step, or visible result that directly represents it instead of a text explanation card;
- for invisible or abstract mechanisms, anchor the visual to a concrete supported object, apparatus, material, location, or visible result rather than an abstract label;
- every query must include enough specific qualifiers to disambiguate ambiguous nouns and keep the search tied to the intended visible subject;
- if a likely visual confusion exists, populate must_not_show only with concrete conflicting subjects or truly mutually exclusive scene states;
- use only locations and arrangements established by the narration or evidence, or intrinsic to the primary object; do not invent a desk, room, outdoor scene, studio, or display just to make an abstract step visual;
- if a scene explains an abstract effect of the preceding physical subject, keep that same concrete subject unless the narration explicitly introduces another visible object;
- must_not_show may exclude a genuinely wrong subject, never the normal setting or a plausible variant of the required primary subject;
- Do not invent a visible person or hand to illustrate an impersonal mechanism explanation. When narration says pressing a cover/button causes a mechanical result without naming an actor, use a clear photo of the same physical device; keep the mechanism action in narration. Preserve a human action when narration explicitly describes a person/hand or instructs the viewer to perform it, and keep any explicitly required human anchor.
- A visual_intent is a strict photographic requirement. When narration explains a short-lived, hidden, or rarely photographed action such as heating, expansion, current flow, or sensing, describe the exact instrument, material, or visible resulting state in visual_intent; leave the action in narration unless a real photograph of that action is realistically searchable. Do not demand the action in visual_intent merely because narration describes it; do not substitute an unrelated object;
- sanity check that at least one plausible real photograph satisfying visual_intent, every must_show, every must_not_show, and query 1 or 2 could exist; if not, simplify unsupported setting/exclusions or choose the supported physical subject without changing the narration;
- never use generic background/context such as background, landscape, scenery, or bare darkness/brightness/shadows in must_not_show; incidental context visible through windows/openings does not invalidate an otherwise correct required subject and setting;
- never replace a technical process with decorative stock imagery;
- do not use filler search words such as stunning, beautiful, scenery, wallpaper, aesthetic, background, cinematic, generic nature, icon, vector, clipart, illustration, diagram, schematic, or infographic;
- never name a media provider, asset ID, URL, filename, or preselected image/video in a shot;
- preferred_media_type must be exactly photo;
- when a concept is invisible or only available as a digital diagram/map, choose a real photographed instrument, observable physical result, or physically photographed display that demonstrates it; never describe a digital map or illustration as a standalone photo;
- every scene must directly advance the answer to TOPIC; evidence-supported side facts are still forbidden when they do not help answer TOPIC;
- do not spend a scene on generic praise, benefits, environmental/economic impact, history, outro, summary, or broad context unless TOPIC explicitly asks for that aspect;
- for how/process/mechanism topics, keep a causal/operational sequence from input or condition through mechanism/components to the final output/result; the last scene must complete that mechanism/result instead of switching to generic benefits;
- each scene must add a distinct necessary fact; never repeat the same causal step or paraphrase another scene merely to fill the required scene count;
- do not add facts that are not supported by the cited evidence IDs; use specific causal details from those sources to reach the planned narration length instead of descriptive filler;
- do not mention evidence IDs in the spoken narration;
- make narration natural, idiomatic, and native-sounding in the requested language for one continuous voiceover; avoid code-switching and words borrowed from another requested-language variant;
- for Ukrainian output, use standard Ukrainian vocabulary only; do not insert Russian lexical forms;
- count the narration words before returning JSON; target exactly 54 words and never return outside 42-66 words; natural cadence and factual meaning take priority over exact count;
- do not underfill the narration: when a character range is supplied, prefer the middle of that range rather than the minimum;

